{
  "id": "Compound::DB01274 - Disease::DOID:2841",
  "verbalization": "Arformoterol, a member of the adrenergic beta-2 agonist class, is implicated in the treatment of asthma through a mechanism that involves the activation of beta-2 receptors. This pathway is supported by multiple evidence chains, all of which converge on the role of beta-2 adrenergic receptors in modulating airway smooth muscle contraction and inflammation. The shared inclusion of Arformoterol within the adrenergic beta-2 agonist category, along with its association with asthma in multiple paths, suggests a consistent biological role in the disease. The lowest common ancestors across these paths—beta-adenosine agonists and phenylethanolamines—further reinforce the central position of adrenergic signaling in asthma pathophysiology. The evidence supports a mechanism where arformoterol exerts its therapeutic effects by activating beta-2 receptors, leading to bronchodilation and reduction of airway inflammation. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of adrenergic signaling in asthma, with arformoterol serving as a relevant agent in this context.",
